"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[16591],{416591:e=>{e.exports='# Auto CDC in Databricks SQL\n\nAuto CDC processes change data capture records from streaming sources, automatically handling out-of-order records and applying inserts, updates, and deletes to a target streaming table. It exists in two environments with different syntax and capabilities. This skill helps you use the right pattern for each context.\n\n## Two Environments, Two Syntax Forms\n\n### Databricks SQL (SQL editor, notebooks)\n\nSingle-statement form — the target table and flow are defined together:\n\n```sql\nCREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE target_table\nFLOW AUTO CDC\nFROM STREAM(source)\nKEYS (keys)\nSEQUENCE BY order_column\n[IGNORE NULL UPDATES]\n[APPLY AS DELETE WHEN condition]\n[APPLY AS TRUNCATE WHEN condition]\n[COLUMNS {column_list | * EXCEPT (except_list)}]\n[STORED AS {SCD TYPE 1 | SCD TYPE 2}]\n[TRACK HISTORY ON {column_list | * EXCEPT (except_list)}];\n```\n\n- Requires Databricks Runtime 17.3+ and a Pro or Serverless SQL warehouse.\n- Currently in Beta.\n- `TBLPROPERTIES(pipelines.channel = "PREVIEW")` is required during Beta.\n\n### Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines (SDP)\n\nTwo-statement form — the target table is declared first, then the flow is defined separately:\n\n```sql\nCREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE target_table;\n\nCREATE FLOW flow_name\nAS AUTO CDC INTO target_table\nFROM source\nKEYS (keys)\nSEQUENCE BY order_column\n[IGNORE NULL UPDATES]\n[APPLY AS DELETE WHEN condition]\n[APPLY AS TRUNCATE WHEN condition]\n[COLUMNS {column_list | * EXCEPT (except_list)}]\n[STORED AS {SCD TYPE 1 | SCD TYPE 2}]\n[TRACK HISTORY ON {column_list | * EXCEPT (except_list)}];\n```\n\n- The flow gets a separate name, allowing multiple flows to write into the same target table.\n- Supports `AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` for periodic snapshot-based CDC (not available in DBSQL).\n\n## Critical Differences\n\n| Aspect                        | DBSQL / Notebooks                                             | Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines                                                 |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Statement form                | Single statement (`CREATE STREAMING TABLE ... FLOW AUTO CDC`) | Two statements (`CREATE STREAMING TABLE` + `CREATE FLOW ... AS AUTO CDC INTO`) |\n| Cross-use                     | The two-statement form does NOT work in DBSQL                 | Both forms work — single-statement and two-statement                           |\n| Flow naming                   | Implicit (no separate flow name)                              | Explicit `flow_name` required                                                  |\n| Multiple flows into one table | Not supported                                                 | Supported — declare one table, create multiple flows                           |\n| `AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT`      | Not supported                                                 | Supported                                                                      |\n| `TBLPROPERTIES`               | Required: `pipelines.channel = "PREVIEW"` during Beta         | Standard pipeline properties                                                   |\n| Compute requirement           | Pro or Serverless SQL warehouse                               | Pipeline cluster                                                               |\n| Runtime requirement           | DBR 17.3+                                                     | Managed by pipeline runtime                                                    |\n\n## Gotchas That Trip People Up\n\n1. **Wrong syntax for the environment.** This is the #1 mistake. If the user is in a SQL editor or notebook, they MUST use the single-statement form. In a Lakeflow pipeline, both forms work, but the two-statement form is preferred because it supports multiple flows into one table. The two-statement form does NOT work in DBSQL.\n\n2. **`STREAM()` only accepts table/view identifiers, not subqueries.** To filter source data, create a temporary view first, then reference it with `STREAM(view_name)`.\n\n3. **Change Data Feed sources need column exclusion.** When reading from a CDF source with `WITH (readChangeFeed=true)`, always exclude the CDF metadata columns: `COLUMNS * EXCEPT (_change_type, _commit_version, _commit_timestamp)`.\n\n4. **`APPLY AS TRUNCATE WHEN` is SCD Type 1 only.** Using it with SCD Type 2 is an error.\n\n5. **SCD Type 2 with explicit schema requires `__START_AT` and `__END_AT` columns.** These must match the data type of the `SEQUENCE BY` field.\n\n6. **`SEQUENCE BY` column must be sortable and non-null.** NULL values in the sequence column are not supported.\n\n7. **Source must be append-only.** Reading changes or deletions to existing records in the source throws an error.\n\n## KEY RULES\n\n- Detect the user\'s environment (SQL editor/notebook vs. pipeline) and use the appropriate syntax form. If unclear, ask.\n- Do NOT mix syntax forms. Never suggest `CREATE FLOW ... AS AUTO CDC INTO` in a notebook context, and never suggest the single-statement form in a pipeline context.\n- If the user asks about snapshot-based CDC, note that `AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` is only available in Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines, not in DBSQL.\n- Default to SCD Type 1 unless the user explicitly needs history tracking.\n- When the user says "create a table with Auto CDC" without specifying a source, use sample data (e.g., `samples.wanderbricks.booking_updates`) to demonstrate the pattern.\n'}}]);
